      "id": 1,
      "text": "The site was planned in 1892 and named Virginia in honor of the home state of many local lumber workers at that time.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Virginia’s Olcott Park showcases open green space, a bandshell for summer concerts, and a stone fountain built in 1937 by the WPA.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Virginia hosts the World’s Largest Floating Loon, a 21‑foot sculpture built in 1982 by William 'Bill' Martin and first floated by the Land of the Loon Festival Committee.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.virginiamn.us/visitor_info/attractions.php",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Virginia is situated on the Mesabi Range, part of northeastern Minnesota’s Iron Range, a region historically known for iron ore mining.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia%2C_Minnesota",
